# Timesheets, leave, and does the week add up?

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:agenda, github: "elixir-tempo/agenda"}
])
```

## What this is

A runnable answer to three things Josh asked for:

* **Timesheets** — consultants record time, billed to clients and allocated to projects.
* **Leave** — the same structure, allocated to a leave type rather than a project.
* **Reconciliation** — both must add up to the expected working hours per period: day, week, month, quarter, and year in both the calendar and the financial sense.

Everything below runs, and every number in the prose came out of the notebook rather than out of my head.

The design document behind it is `plans/timesheets.md` in this repository. `elixir-tempo/agenda` is private, so the setup cell above resolves against your own GitHub credentials — you have access as a collaborator.

## Setup

Two helpers. `business_hours/1` builds a working pattern over a window — Monday to Friday, 09:00–13:00 and 14:00–17:36. That is **7.6 hours a day and 38 a week**, the Australian full-time standard, and a number chosen to embarrass anyone storing hours as a float.

`hours/1` is for display only.

```elixir
import Tempo.Sigils

alias Agenda.Reconciliation

pad = fn number -> String.pad_leading("#{number}", 2, "0") end

business_hours = fn window ->
  {:ok, workdays} = Tempo.select(window, Tempo.workdays(:AU))

  workdays
  |> Tempo.IntervalSet.to_list()
  |> Enum.flat_map(fn day ->
    date = "#{day.from.time[:year]}-#{pad.(day.from.time[:month])}-#{pad.(day.from.time[:day])}"

    [
      Tempo.from_iso8601!("#{date}T09:00:00/#{date}T13:00:00"),
      Tempo.from_iso8601!("#{date}T14:00:00/#{date}T17:36:00")
    ]
  end)
  |> Tempo.IntervalSet.new!()
end

hours = fn set ->
  {_count, duration} = Agenda.Limit.total(Tempo.IntervalSet.to_list(set))
  {:ok, value} = Tempo.Duration.to_unit(duration, :hour)
  Float.round(value, 2)
end

:ready
```

Note what `Tempo.workdays(:AU)` did there: weekends are territory-specific and the library knows them. Holidays are **not** in that call, and the section below is about why.

## The consultant is a resource with a contract

A person and a meeting room are the same kind of thing here; only the attributes differ. The working pattern is availability, and the contract is a limit.

```elixir
quarter = ~o"2026-07-01/2026-10-01"

{:ok, dana} =
  Agenda.resource("Dana",
    skills: [:elixir, :postgres],
    limits: [day: ~o"PT7H36M"]
  )
  |> Agenda.open(business_hours.(quarter))

%{blocks: Tempo.IntervalSet.count(dana.open), hours: hours.(dana.open)}
```

132 blocks — a morning and an afternoon on each of 66 working days — totalling 501.6 hours, which is `66 × 7.6`. Nobody wrote 66; it fell out of the calendar, and neither weekends nor the lunch break needed a rule. **Lunch is not a constraint to enforce; it is simply time Dana is not open**, and every calculation downstream inherits that for free.

The limit is a **ceiling measured in time**, not in bookings:

```elixir
dana.limits
```

## A timesheet entry is a tagged claim

This is the load-bearing decision, and it is Josh's: a timesheet line and a leave line are the same structure, separated by what the time was *for*.

```elixir
enter = fn ledger, name, interval, tag ->
  arrangement = %Agenda.Arrangement{
    session: name,
    interval: Tempo.from_iso8601!(interval),
    allocations: %{consultant: [dana]}
  }

  {:ok, ledger} = Agenda.allocate(ledger, arrangement, tag: tag)
  ledger
end

full_day = fn ledger, day, name, tag ->
  ledger
  |> enter.("#{name} #{day} am", "2026-08-#{day}T09:00:00/2026-08-#{day}T13:00:00", tag)
  |> enter.("#{name} #{day} pm", "2026-08-#{day}T14:00:00/2026-08-#{day}T17:36:00", tag)
end

acme = {:project, "ACME-2026-01"}
beta = {:project, "BETA-2026-04"}
annual = {:leave, :annual}

:ready
```

`agenda` does not know what a project or a leave type is, and does not decide which kinds exist. What it gains is that **a day cannot be two things** — billing a client while on annual leave is two claims on one person over one span, which is the overlap the ledger already refuses. That is not a validation rule somebody has to remember to write.

## A week that adds up

Monday 10 to Friday 14 August 2026. Two days on Acme, two on Beta, one on leave.

```elixir
week = ~o"2026-08-10/2026-08-15"

ledger =
  Agenda.ledger()
  |> full_day.("10", "Acme", acme)
  |> full_day.("11", "Acme", acme)
  |> full_day.("12", "Beta", beta)
  |> full_day.("13", "Beta", beta)
  |> full_day.("14", "Leave", annual)

{:ok, report} = Agenda.reconcile(ledger, dana, within: week)

%{
  balanced?: Reconciliation.balanced?(report),
  expected: hours.(report.expected),
  claimed: hours.(report.claimed),
  breaches: report.breaches
}
```

And where the week went:

```elixir
report.by_tag
|> Enum.map(fn {tag, duration} ->
  {:ok, value} = Tempo.Duration.to_unit(duration, :hour)
  {tag, Float.round(value, 2)}
end)
|> Enum.sort()
```

## Holidays are somebody else's problem

Josh's 🤯 about Queensland show days is the right reaction. Under the *Holidays Act 1983* (Qld) a show holiday is appointed **per district**, so Brisbane's falls on the Ekka Wednesday while Toowoomba's, Cairns's and Townsville's fall on entirely different dates. Australia Day is national, Labour Day is per state on different dates, Easter Sunday is a holiday in some states and not others.

**None of that is this library's business.** Accurate sub-national holiday data is a maintenance problem with a far wider audience than scheduling, so holidays arrive as an ordinary interval set from wherever you resolve them — a jurisdiction library, a CalDAV feed through `Agenda.from_ical/1`, or a hand-written list.

Brisbane's show holiday falls on the Wednesday of the Ekka, and 12 August 2026 is a Wednesday. The date here is illustrative — a real system would take it from the district's proclaimed calendar. The mechanism is not illustrative.

```elixir
{:ok, show_day} = Tempo.IntervalSet.new([~o"2026-08-12/2026-08-13"])

{:ok, plain} = Agenda.reconcile(Agenda.ledger(), dana, within: week)
{:ok, holiday} = Agenda.reconcile(Agenda.ledger(), dana, within: week, excluding: show_day)

%{without_holiday: hours.(plain.expected), with_show_day: hours.(holiday.expected)}
```

> *"A week is 38 hours. The week of the Ekka is 30.4."*

The consultant's calendar and the client site's calendar are **different axes**. A Sydney-employed consultant on a Brisbane site during the Ekka cannot get into the building — an availability fact about the site resource — and still owes a normal working day. Because holidays are just data attached to whichever resource they belong to, that needs no mechanism at all.

## The trap: a holiday inside a period of leave

Dana takes the whole of that week off. Only four days are owed, so only four are drawn from her leave balance — never five.

```elixir
on_leave =
  Enum.reduce(["10", "11", "13", "14"], Agenda.ledger(), &full_day.(&2, &1, "Leave", annual))

{:ok, leave_report} = Agenda.reconcile(on_leave, dana, within: week, excluding: show_day)
{:ok, drawn} = Tempo.Duration.to_unit(leave_report.by_tag[annual], :hour)

%{
  balanced?: Reconciliation.balanced?(leave_report),
  leave_drawn: Float.round(drawn, 2),
  unaccounted: hours.(leave_report.unaccounted)
}
```

This is the perennial payroll bug, and here it is **unrepresentable** rather than guarded against. Holidays leave the expectation *before* claims are compared against it, so a leave request can only ever consume days that were owed in the first place. The counter-based version of this code is where the bug lives.

## The case a sum cannot see

Here is the argument for doing this as set algebra rather than arithmetic.

Dana misses the Monday and works the Saturday. Same hours. Different week.

```elixir
wrong =
  ["11", "13", "14"]
  |> Enum.reduce(Agenda.ledger(), &full_day.(&2, &1, "Acme", acme))
  |> full_day.("15", "Acme", acme)

{:ok, wrong_report} =
  Agenda.reconcile(wrong, dana, within: ~o"2026-08-10/2026-08-16", excluding: show_day)

%{
  expected: hours.(wrong_report.expected),
  claimed: hours.(wrong_report.claimed),
  totals_match: hours.(wrong_report.expected) == hours.(wrong_report.claimed),
  balanced?: Reconciliation.balanced?(wrong_report)
}
```

**The totals match exactly and the week is still wrong.** A check that sums hours and compares to a number passes on this data. `unaccounted` and `overclaimed` are interval sets rather than quantities, so they say *which* time is missing — which is what a person can act on:

```elixir
Reconciliation.explain(wrong_report)
```

## Limits catch the day that ran long

A ten-hour Monday, against a 7.6-hour ceiling:

```elixir
crunch = %Agenda.Arrangement{
  session: "Acme crunch",
  interval: ~o"2026-08-10T09:00:00/2026-08-10T19:00:00",
  allocations: %{consultant: [dana]}
}

{:ok, long_day} = Agenda.allocate(Agenda.ledger(), crunch, tag: acme)
{:ok, long_report} = Agenda.reconcile(long_day, dana, within: ~o"2026-08-10/2026-08-11")

Reconciliation.explain(long_report)
```

Two separate findings from one entry, and the first is the subtle one: working through the 13:00–14:00 break is time claimed against hours the contract never offered.

## An open question about floors

A limit may set a floor as well as a ceiling. Only ceilings constrain scheduling — a partial layout is *supposed* to be under its floor — so a floor is checked here, by reconciliation, and nowhere else.

But watch what an **absolute** weekly floor does in a week containing a public holiday:

```elixir
{:ok, contracted} =
  Agenda.resource("Dana", limits: [day: ~o"PT7H36M", week: [at_least: ~o"PT38H"]])
  |> Agenda.open(business_hours.(quarter))

worked =
  Enum.reduce(["10", "11", "13", "14"], Agenda.ledger(), &full_day.(&2, &1, "Acme", acme))

{:ok, floored} = Agenda.reconcile(worked, contracted, within: week, excluding: show_day)

%{
  unaccounted: hours.(floored.unaccounted),
  overclaimed: hours.(floored.overclaimed),
  breaches: Reconciliation.explain(floored)
}
```

Dana worked every hour she owed — `unaccounted` and `overclaimed` are both zero — and the 38-hour floor still reports a breach, because the show day means only 30.4 hours were ever owed.

**This is a question rather than a defect, and it is Alembic's to answer.** Is a weekly floor an absolute contractual constant, in which case a holiday week genuinely under-runs it and the report is right? Or is it a proportion of the hours actually owed, in which case the floor should be prorated against `expected` and this library should offer that? The current behaviour is the literal reading. Say which you want and it is a small change.

## The financial year

The common implementation keeps Gregorian dates and adds six months wherever a report says "FY". Tempo's answer is that a financial year is a **calendar**, so fiscal and Gregorian values sit on one timeline.

```elixir
{:ok, calendar} = Calendrical.FiscalYear.calendar_for(:AU)
{:ok, fy26} = Tempo.from_iso8601("2026", calendar)
{:ok, months} = Tempo.to_interval(fy26)

{Enum.count(months), months |> Enum.at(0) |> Tempo.to_iso8601()}
```

`"2026Y1M"` is a *fiscal* label, not a Gregorian date, and this is the trap worth running rather than reasoning about:

```elixir
Tempo.relation(Tempo.from_iso8601!("2026-01-01", calendar), ~o"2025-07-01")
```

**Australian FY2026 begins in July 2025.** So "FY26" and "2026" name overlapping but different stretches of time, and any report carrying both has to agree with its reader about which calendar year a financial year starts in. The projection settles it, rather than a convention nobody wrote down.

The territory data is already there — `1 July - 30 June` for `AU` — so the whole day/week/month/quarter/year ladder is one mechanism rather than five. Each is a granule of some calendar, and the same set algebra runs at every level:

```elixir
{:ok, q1} = Agenda.reconcile(Agenda.ledger(), dana, within: quarter)
{:ok, workdays} = Tempo.select(quarter, Tempo.workdays(:AU))

%{
  working_days: Tempo.IntervalSet.count(workdays),
  hours_owed: hours.(q1.expected)
}
```

That is Q1 of FY2027 — July to September 2026 — before any holiday is subtracted.

## Where this stops

Three things were built in `agenda` for this, and all three are useful to every existing user without mentioning timesheets:

* `Agenda.Allocation`'s `:tag` — what a claim was for.
* `Agenda.Limit` — limits measuring time as well as claims, with floors as well as ceilings.
* `Agenda.reconcile/3` — the report above.

Two things deliberately stayed out:

* **Holiday resolution.** The jurisdiction tree, effective-dated calendars, and the data behind them. Its own library, with its own maintainers, feeding an interval set in.
* **Leave balances.** Accrual, carry-over caps, cash-out, leave loading. That is payroll, none of it expressible as interval algebra, and it belongs in Alembic's application.

## Questions for Josh

1. **Is a timesheet entry placed or unplaced?** *"Six hours on Acme on Tuesday"* is a quantity in a bucket; *"09:00–15:00 on Acme"* is an interval. Everything above uses intervals, and only intervals can detect that a consultant was double-booked. Which do consultants actually type?
2. **Absolute or prorated floors?** The open question above.
3. **What granularity is billed?** Six-minute units are conventional, and interact with the 7.6-hour day in ways worth deciding once, in decimal, up front.
4. **Can a closed period be reopened?** If a holiday is proclaimed late, does reconciliation re-run and restate? This decides whether a closed period must record which calendar version it was closed against.
5. **Half-days.** A half-day of personal leave plus a half-day billed is the smallest case that breaks a day-granular model.
6. **Time zones.** Perth to Sydney is two hours, three in summer. Tempo handles it given a real IANA database configured — Elixir's default is UTC-only and makes DST arithmetic silently wrong rather than failing.
